 "title": "Why You Need Narcissistic Confidence in Conversations",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that words have a spell-like effect, meaning you must be mindful of how you talk to yourself. To handle tough situations and grow, you should treat your brain as a pattern recognition machine and adopt narcissistic tendencies instead of fearing judgment.",
 "key_points": [
  "Treat your brain as a pattern recognition machine instead of assuming new situations are entirely unprecedented.",
  "Adopt narcissistic tendencies during conversations so you do not fear the judgment of others.",
  "Average people fear being judged, whereas a narcissist thinks, 'I want to speak what I want to speak... if you like it okay, if you don't like it okay as well.'",
  "Without an 'F U' attitude towards situations and the world, you will not be able to grow.",
  "Your brain loves familiar things and will always choose a familiar hell over an unfamiliar heaven.",
  "Instead of telling your brain a challenge is totally new and being scared, remind it of past similar experiences to calm your nerves down."
 ],
 "content": "The creator starts by citing Jerry Seinfeld on how words have a spell, which is why you must be mindful of how you talk to yourself. She shares two main rules: treat your brain as a pattern recognition machine, and have narcissistic tendencies while speaking to yourself. She claims everyone needs narcissistic tendencies because humans face difficult situations like changing cities or building from scratch that require massive confidence, reaching a point where it becomes narcissism. She uses her consulting experience on Topmate as an example, where clients say words do not come out of their mouths because they lack confidence. She tells them to 'be a narcissist' because average people fear judgment, while a narcissist thinks the world revolves around them and gets their way anyway. She clarifies she does not mean in personal relationships, but the world is brutal and a 'doggy dog [sic] competition'. When starting a business, if a friend cancels, a narcissist says 'F U' and goes ahead because they know they will figure it out based on past experience, whereas an average person is jittery and nervous. She states, 'You can take life by its neck once you know how to be a narcissist.' Next, she explains why your brain is a pattern recognition machine: your brain loves familiarity and chooses a familiar hell over an unfamiliar heaven. When facing a new city, tough exam, or hard conversation, people wrongly tell their brains they are scared because it is the first time. Instead, you should tell your brain that this is not new, reminding it of past exams, negotiations, or anxiety. When you reference past experiences, your anxiety drops from high to low because your brain has something to reference back to, which calms your nerves. Finally, she directs viewers to a link in her description to find out what kind of communicator they are.",
